class Solution:
    def longestSemiRepetitiveSubstring(self, s: str) -> int:
        if len(s) <= 1:
            return len(s)

        max_length = 1
        start = 0
        first_pair_index = -1

        for end in range(1, len(s)):
            if s[end] == s[end - 1]:
                if first_pair_index != -1:
                    start = first_pair_index + 1
                first_pair_index = end - 1
            max_length = max(max_length, end - start + 1)

        return max_length
```

This solution uses a sliding window approach to find the longest semi-repetitive substring. It keeps track of the start of the current window and the index of the first pair of adjacent identical digits encountered. When a second pair is found, it adjusts the start of the window to be just after the first pair, ensuring that the substring remains semi-repetitive. The maximum length of such substrings is updated and returned.

